-- Source module: Solutions.KFBQop
/-!
# KFB: the two-stream pass `qop` (fine cost)

On a Gaussian array viewed as consecutive blocks `[u ‖ v]` of two streams of `B1` entries each,
`qop Ms` applies the small `2 × 2` Gaussian matrix `M_p = Ms[p mod L]` (`L = |Ms|`) to block `p`:
`(u, v) ↦ (M₀₀ u + M₀₁ v, M₁₀ u + M₁₁ v)` entrywise.  With a single matrix this is a
selected-bit kernel on a field `[2]` (the `C` kernel (25) scaled by 2, a phase on the selected
half, a sign); with the period `[1, X]` it is a CNOT whose control is the field just before.

Per block: one move of the block to a scratch stack (reversal), the two streams distributed to
four register stacks (real/imaginary parts), one zip loop computing the outputs with linear
register expressions (`lcE`), the second output stream moved back in order.  Every entry is
touched `O(1)` times; loop counters are unary (`fcode`) with overhead linear in the counts.

Stacks: data `0`; scratch `qT = 33`, output accumulator `qA = 34`, `qO = 35`; stream registers
`36 … 39`; counters `30, 31`; counts `48 … 52` (`G`, `B1`, `2B1`, `4B1`, total items).
-/

