section
-- Source module: Solutions.R6BitNet
/-!
# Round six, bit side: interchange networks with general frames (`QHNet`)

The array-model notion of network needed by S. Jain's round six (ARCH.md §2 R1, design
decision 2). Compared with `AModel.Circuit` (OpenAI Prop. 11):

* there is no `unit` restriction and no shear structure: every **edge** is an explicit frame
  change on one stream, given by Bruhat data `E₁ · σ · E₂` with rational `2m × 2m` lower
  triangular factors (reduced mod the field size `N`) and a permutation `σ` that is a product of
  field transpositions `H_{i+k} ↔ D_{j+k}`, grouped into **runs** `(i, j, r)` (`k < r`);
  each run is executed as **one** recursive child interchange of width `r` fields (so child widths
  are arbitrary `0 < r < m`, as in the partial-swap Bruhat run decomposition);
* **gates** are arbitrary pointwise linear maps on a set of wires (copies, XORs, the copy
  correction, the scalar word);
* wires are `Fin W ⊕ Fin T`: `W` roles (one per row residue of the row split) and `T`
  temporaries (initialised to `0`, discarded at the end);
* correctness (`QHNet.IdealOK N`) is stated for the **ideal** execution, in which every edge runs
  its Bruhat steps exactly (`runArr`), on streams of *any* shape with *any* layout of `2m` active
  fields of size `N`: role `ρ w` ends with the chunk interchange `amap swapMat` of the input
  stream `w`. This is the hypothesis later workers prove for the real network (e.g. from
  `AModel.linear_via_bruhat_array` and a frame-label invariant);
* `hist`: the child-width histogram `[(r, 1)]`, one entry per run of every edge.
-/

set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false

